ularly in popular culture, include depiction of butterflies. Collin responds by saying that these butterflies are weird and finds a piece of paper to try drawing one.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#1ABC9C\">Collin\u2019s lack of engagement in the mythology or symbolism of the butterfly meant Elias did not have the opportunity to talk about how butterflies were associated with the soul in Greek mythology, hence Psyche (goddess of the soul) often being depicted with butterfly wings. In fact, the word psyche was used for both the word soul and butterfly.</span><br /> <br /> Damian, professional investigator and designated adult of the group, manages to get a lot more information out of Elias than Derrik did. The group learns that Jacob was indeed rather troubled, but that the source of his mental state seemed to be a year-long assignment for the class Elias taught. Students were expected to either analyze a classical myth and reinterpret it through a modern lens and appropriate symbolism or to explore and analyze modern folklore. Elias says that he became fixated on the project, and even seeing a therapist did nothing to help his mental state. Derrik raises his hands and asks more about the therapist, but Elias doesn\u2019t seem to have much to provide, except that she was a she, and that Jacob mentioned that she wore glasses. Derrik immediately puts his face in his hands.<br /> <br /> <i>&quot;Fucking stalker.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> It quickly becomes apparent that Derrik is convinced Jacob was seeing his therapist. Damian and Elias seem skeptical, given that &quot;a woman who wears glasses&quot; is a relatively common descriptor, but Derrik is adamant. In fact, he's certain of it. Almost as if on cue, he hears the ping of an incoming text on his burner phone, the source of which is that selfsame therapist.<br /> <br /> Therapist: Derek. We need to talk. I see that you're at UCLA. We should have a session ASAP.<br /> <br /> Collin finishes his artistic rendering of